              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="param efd-obs-only" data-obs-for="ldos" hidden>
              <div class="param-header">
                <label>Peaks tracked</label>
                <input type="number" class="param-number" id="efd-npeaks" value="3" step="1" min="1" max="6">
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="param efd-obs-only" data-obs-for="nearfield" hidden>
              <span class="efd-src-note">
                The map is at one energy, set on the slider above the map. Put it
                on a resonance &mdash; the Modes view gives you the band
                energies, and Material shows where
                <span set:html={im('\\mathrm{Re}\\,\\eta')} /> meets them.
              </span>
            </div>
            <div class="param efd-obs-only efd-param-pair" data-obs-for="nearfield" hidden>
              <label>
                <span>Points along x</span>
                <input type="number" class="param-number" id="efd-nf-nx" value="140" step="10" min="2" max="300">
              </label>
              <label>
                <span>Points along z</span>
                <input type="number" class="param-number" id="efd-nf-nz" value="84" step="6" min="2" max="300">
              </label>
            </div>

            <!-- ===== Environment =====
                 eps below and a substrate stack are two descriptions of the SAME
                 physics and the library does not stop you giving both: eps_b
                 enters only through eps_eff = (eps_a + eps_b)/2, which scales
                 every eta, while the stack screens that same interface again
                 through r(Q) inside Vbar. Giving both screens it twice and
                 nothing raises. So they are offered as alternatives: a substrate
                 epsilon above zero takes over, and eps below is hidden and
                 pinned to eps above. The library's own validation message says
                 the same thing ("use eps_a = eps_b = layers[0].eps_par"). -->
            <h4 class="efd-group-title">Environment</h4>
            <div class="param efd-param-pair">
              <label>
                <span>&epsilon; above</span>
                <input type="number" class="param-number" id="efd-eps-a" value="1" step="0.1" min="1">
              </label>
