            <p class="efd-readout">
              <span>Estimate <b id="efd-out-cost">&mdash;</b></span>
              <span>Route <b id="efd-out-route">&mdash;</b></span>
              <span>Headline <b id="efd-out-headline">&mdash;</b> <i id="efd-out-headline-unit"></i></span>
            </p>

            <div class="export-bar">
              <span class="export-label">Export the geometry</span>
              <button id="efd-geo-png" class="export-btn" type="button" title="Download the geometry drawing as PNG">PNG</button>
              <button id="efd-geo-csv" class="export-btn" type="button" title="Download every node coordinate as CSV">CSV</button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="notes-card card">
            <h4>Notes</h4>
            <p class="theory-note">
              <strong>One self-consistent problem.</strong> Every structure here is
              a two-dimensional sheet whose induced charge responds to the total
              potential, external plus induced. Discretising the sheet on
              <span set:html={im('L')} /> nodes turns that into a linear system
            </p>
            <div class="note-eq" set:html={eq('\\vec\\phi = \\big[\\mathbb{1}-(\\mathcal{M}+\\bar{\\mathcal{M}})\\big]^{-1}\\vec\\phi^{\\,\\rm ext}, \\qquad \\mathcal{M}_{jj\'} = \\mathcal{V}^{(q)}_{jj\'}\\,\\mathcal{D}^{(q)}_{j\'}')} />
            <p class="theory-note">
              in which <span set:html={im('\\mathcal{V}^{(q)}')} /> is the Coulomb
              kernel between node cells, <span set:html={im('\\mathcal{D}^{(q)}')} />
              the finite-difference second derivative, and
              <span set:html={im('\\bar{\\mathcal{M}}')} /> the reflected interaction
              a layered substrate adds. All the material physics sits in one
              dimensionless number,
              <span set:html={im('\\eta(\\omega)=\\mathrm{i}\\sigma(\\omega)/\\omega W\\bar\\varepsilon')} />,
              so the eigenvalues of the geometric operator are
              <em>material-independent</em> and the resonances are wherever
              <span set:html={im('\\eta(\\omega)=\\eta_j')} />. That is why
              <strong>Modes</strong> and <strong>Dispersion</strong> are offered
              under every excitation: they are properties of the structure, not of
              how you drive it.
            </p>

            <h4>Drift is not a Doppler shift</h4>
            <p class="theory-note">
              A drift current is carried by a <em>skewed</em> Fermi&ndash;Dirac
              distribution, and solving the Boltzmann equation on it gives an
              anisotropic, nonlocal conductivity tensor &mdash; with
              <span set:html={im('\\alpha = v_{\\rm F}Q/(\\omega+\\mathrm{i}\\gamma)')} />
              and <span set:html={im('\\beta = v_{\\rm d}/v_{\\rm F}')} />,
            </p>
            <div class="note-eq" set:html={eq('\\sigma_\\parallel(Q)=\\frac{2\\sigma_\\omega}{(\\alpha+\\beta)^2}\\left(\\frac{1+\\alpha\\beta}{\\sqrt{1-\\alpha^2}}-\\sqrt{1-\\beta^2}\\right)')} />
            <p class="theory-note">
              Three things a frame shift would get wrong, and this does not: the
              drift survives <span set:html={im('Q\\to 0')} />, so it renormalises
              even the plane-wave response; the non-reciprocity lives in
              <span set:html={im('(\\alpha+\\beta)^2')} /> and the
              <span set:html={im('\\alpha\\beta')} /> cross term, so
              <span set:html={im('q\\to-q')} /> is a symmetry only at
              <span set:html={im('\\beta=0')} />; and
              <span set:html={im('\\eta')} /> becomes a tensor, which is why
              <strong>Dispersion</strong> refuses a drifted run and sends you to the
              LDOS map &mdash; a single eigenvalue spectrum then has no resonance
              condition to invert.
            </p>

            <h4>What the numbers mean</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                The Dirac cone is electron&ndash;hole symmetric, so every
                conductivity takes <span set:html={im('|E_{\\rm F}|')} /> and hole
                doping gives exactly the electron-doped answer. Signed formulas
                would return <span set:html={im('\\mathrm{Re}\\,\\sigma<0')} /> &mdash;
                gain from a passive sheet.
              </li>
              <li>
                Eigenvalues are <em>geometric</em>. The same
                <span set:html={im('\\eta_j')} /> come out under any conductivity
                convention, so they cannot be used to pin one. The tool hides the
                operator's charge-neutrality null mode, whose
                <span set:html={im('\\eta')} /> diverges; it is an artefact, not a
                resonance.
              </li>
              <li>
                The LDOS is in <strong>arbitrary units</strong>. Read peak positions
                and relative weights.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <PresetStrip presets={presets} />
          </div>

          <div class="references card">
            <h4>References</h4>
            <ul class="reference-list">
              <li><span class="ref-index">[1]</span><span>T. P. Rasmussen, A. Rodr&iacute;guez Echarri, F. J. Garc&iacute;a de Abajo &amp; J. D. Cox, <em>Nonlocal and cascaded effects in nonlinear graphene nanoplasmonics</em>, Nanoscale <strong>15</strong>, 3150 (2023).</span></li>
              <li><span class="ref-index">[2]</span><span>T. P. Rasmussen, A. Rodr&iacute;guez Echarri, J. D. Cox &amp; F. J. Garc&iacute;a de Abajo, <em>Generation of entangled waveguided photon pairs by free electrons</em>, Sci. Adv. <strong>10</strong>, eadn6312 (2024).</span></li>
              <li><span class="ref-index">[3]</span><span>A. Rodr&iacute;guez Echarri, F. J. Garc&iacute;a de Abajo &amp; J. D. Cox, <em>Nonreciprocal plasmons in one-dimensional carbon nanostructures</em>, Nat. Commun. <strong>17</strong>, 1114 (2026), <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-67872-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">doi:10.1038/s41467-025-67872-5</a>.</span></li>
              <li><span class="ref-index">[4]</span><span>Cox, Yu &amp; Garc&iacute;a de Abajo, Phys. Rev. B <strong>96</strong>, 045442 (2017) &mdash; the nonlinear sheet conductivities.</span></li>
              <li><span class="ref-index">[5]</span><span>Yu, Cox, Garc&iacute;a de Abajo <em>et&nbsp;al.</em>, ACS Photonics <strong>4</strong>, 3106 (2017) &mdash; the coupled-ribbon geometry one example uses. The only evidence tying those parameters to that paper's Fig.&nbsp;5c is a comment in the reference code, so read the example as the parameters the reference attributes to it.</span></li>
              <li><span class="ref-index">[6]</span><span>G. W. Hanson, <em>Dyadic Green's functions and guided surface waves for a surface conductivity model of graphene</em>, J. Appl. Phys. <strong>103</strong>, 064302 (2008) &mdash; the interband logarithm.</span></li>
              <li><span class="ref-index">[7]</span><span>M. Jablan, H. Buljan &amp; M. Soljačić, <em>Plasmonics in graphene at infrared frequencies</em>, Phys. Rev. B <strong>80</strong>, 245435 (2009) &mdash; the 2D plasmon dispersion the sheet pole reproduces.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Every field component at once, after the cylinder page's mode-field
           dialog. The components share one symmetric scale so their relative
           sizes are readable; |E| keeps its own, because it is positive. -->
      <dialog class="efd-dialog efd-field-dialog" id="efd-field-dialog" aria-labelledby="efd-field-dialog-title">
        <div class="efd-dialog__panel">
          <div class="efd-dialog__header">
            <div>
              <h3 id="efd-field-dialog-title">Induced field, every component</h3>
              <span class="efd-dialog__meta" id="efd-field-meta"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="efd-dialog__actions">
              <button type="button" class="chart-tool-btn" id="efd-field-log" title="Toggle a log scale on |E|">Log |E|</button>
              <button type="button" class="structure-dialog__close" id="efd-field-dialog-close" aria-label="Close the field view">&times;</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="efd-field-grid">
            <div class="field-tile" style="grid-area:rex"><div class="field-tile__header"><span class="field-tile__label">Re E<sub>x</sub></span></div><canvas id="efd-f-ex-re" aria-label="Re Ex"></canvas></div>
            <div class="field-tile" style="grid-area:rey"><div class="field-tile__header"><span class="field-tile__label">Re E<sub>y</sub></span></div><canvas id="efd-f-ey-re" aria-label="Re Ey"></canvas></div>
            <div class="field-tile" style="grid-area:rez"><div class="field-tile__header"><span class="field-tile__label">Re E<sub>z</sub></span></div><canvas id="efd-f-ez-re" aria-label="Re Ez"></canvas></div>
            <div class="field-tile" style="grid-area:imx"><div class="field-tile__header"><span class="field-tile__label">Im E<sub>x</sub></span></div><canvas id="efd-f-ex-im" aria-label="Im Ex"></canvas></div>
            <div class="field-tile" style="grid-area:imy"><div class="field-tile__header"><span class="field-tile__label">Im E<sub>y</sub></span></div><canvas id="efd-f-ey-im" aria-label="Im Ey"></canvas></div>
            <div class="field-tile" style="grid-area:imz"><div class="field-tile__header"><span class="field-tile__label">Im E<sub>z</sub></span></div><canvas id="efd-f-ez-im" aria-label="Im Ez"></canvas></div>
            <div class="field-tile" style="grid-area:mag"><div class="field-tile__header"><span class="field-tile__label">|E|</span></div><canvas id="efd-f-mag" aria-label="Field magnitude"></canvas></div>
          </div>
          <p class="efd-dialog__caption">
            The induced field only, on an arbitrary common scale. Black marks the
            ribbons; a dot marks a dipole and a dashed line an electron's path.
          </p>
        </div>
      </dialog>

      <!-- Enlarged geometry, following the dispersion dialog on the cylinder page. -->
      <dialog class="efd-dialog" id="efd-schematic-dialog" aria-labelledby="efd-schematic-dialog-title">
        <div class="efd-dialog__panel">
          <div class="efd-dialog__header">
            <h3 id="efd-schematic-dialog-title">Geometry</h3>
            <button type="button" class="structure-dialog__close" id="efd-schematic-dialog-close" aria-label="Close the enlarged geometry">&times;</button>
          </div>
          <div class="efd-dialog__canvas-box"><canvas id="efd-schematic-large" aria-label="Geometry, enlarged"></canvas></div>
          <p class="efd-dialog__caption" id="efd-schematic-caption">
            Each ribbon is drawn as its line of discretisation nodes. The sheets are
            two-dimensional and carry no thickness; the vertical axis is to the same
            scale as the horizontal one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </dialog>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .module-bar { --module-cols: 3; }
  .excitation-param { padding-bottom: var(--space-xs); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bg-muted); }

  /* The shared `.controls` rhythm is 1rem between every block, and a group
     heading added its own 1rem on top of that -- so a Source heading sat 36px
     from the control above it and the section ran far taller than it had any
     content for. Scoped to this page; lab.css keeps its own spacing for the
     labs that want it. */
  .controls { gap: 0.7rem; }
  .efd-group-title {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0.1rem;
    padding-top: 0.45rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bg-muted);
  }
  .efd-sub-title {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  /* One block per ribbon, as a card rather than a left rule: each now holds
     two sub-sections (Geometry, Material) and a bare rule did not contain them
     visibly enough to read as one ribbon. Shaped after the heterostructure
     lab's .layer-row so the two pages read alike. */
  /* A flex column with a gap, not a bare block. As a block the card had NO
     rhythm of its own: two consecutive .param rows sat flush at 0px, so the
     gamma/beta/T labels touched the E_F slider above them, while the gaps that
     did exist came from the sub-titles' margins and ran 3px / 5px / 8px with
     no pattern. One gap for every row, and a bigger step above a section
     heading than below it, so a heading reads as belonging to what follows. */
  .efd-ribbon-group {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--bg-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--bg-soft);
    padding: 0.45rem var(--space-sm) 0.55rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }
  /* Geometry on the left, "Ribbon N" on the right, one line. */
  .efd-ribbon-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .efd-ribbon-name {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .efd-ribbon-group .efd-sub-title {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    /* 0.35rem of gap + this = the step before a heading; the gap alone is the
       step after it. Shorthand on purpose: it also clears the base rule's
       margin-bottom, which the gap now provides. */
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
  }
  .efd-ribbon-head .efd-sub-title { margin: 0; }

  /* The count is one short value, so it belongs beside its label rather than
     under it; .param-select is width:100% and would otherwise span the column. */
  .efd-count-row { margin-bottom: var(--space-xs); }
  .efd-count-select { width: auto; min-width: 5rem; }

  /* A substrate stack, borrowed from the heterostructure lab. The rows are
     injected with innerHTML, so they carry no Astro scope attribute and every
     class used inside one must ALSO exist as :global() or it styles nothing. */
  /* In the range block's header, beside the numbers it describes. */
  .efd-axis-pick { display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .efd-axis-pick[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* electron.astro's .sp-range, in this page's own tokens: a header carrying
     the quantity and its axis toggle, then "min to max" on one line. */
  /* Centred over the empty plot area, and never in the way of a drawn one:
     it only ever shows while the canvas is blank. */
  .efd-needs-run {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0 var(--space-md);
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .efd-needs-run[hidden] { display: none; }

  .efd-range { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.3rem; }
  /* .param and .efd-range are both display:flex, author rules, so each needs
     its own [hidden] restated -- the UA rule loses to both. */
  .efd-e-grid[hidden] { display: none; }
  .efd-range__head {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .efd-range__head > label { font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--text); }
  .efd-range__row { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem; }
  .efd-range__row > .efd-rnum { flex: 1 1 0; width: auto; min-width: 0; }
  .efd-range__dash { font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--text-muted); }

  /* The map's energy, beside the map. margin-left:auto pushes it to the far end
     of the toolbar, as .angular-controls does on the Mie page. */
  /* `flex: 0 1 auto`, not `1 1 14rem`. Growing to fill handed every spare
     pixel to the slider, which ran most of the toolbar's width while "E =" was
     squeezed hard enough to break across two lines. The box takes what it
     needs and `margin-left: auto` still puts it at the far end. */
  .efd-nf-energy-box {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    margin-left: auto;
    flex: 0 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  /* The symbols are the part that must never be squeezed: they are two or
     three characters and they carry the units. */
  .efd-nf-energy-box > label,
  .efd-nf-energy-box > span { flex: 0 0 auto; white-space: nowrap; }
  /* Same automatic-minimum trap as .efd-slider-num above: without min-width 0
     the number box cannot shrink below its 20-character intrinsic size. */
  .efd-nf-energy-box > .param-number { flex: 0 0 4rem; width: 4rem; min-width: 0; }
  .efd-nf-energy-box > .efd-nf-energy-slider {
    flex: 0 1 9rem;
    width: 9rem;
    min-width: 4rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .efd-nf-energy-box > .efd-nf-lambda { flex: 0 0 4.6rem; width: 4.6rem; min-width: 0; }
  #efd-chart-aux-block[hidden] { display: none; }

  .efd-layer-editor { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-xs); }
  .efd-stack[hidden], .efd-layer-editor[hidden] { display: none; }
  :global(.efd-layer-row) {
    border: 1px solid var(--bg-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: var(--space-xs) var(--space-sm);
  }
  :global(.efd-layer-chip) {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  /* `minmax(0, 1fr)`, not `1fr`. A bare `1fr` is `minmax(auto, 1fr)`, and its
     auto floor is the content's minimum -- which for `.param-number`'s fixed
     5rem is 80px. Three of those plus the gaps came to 248px inside a 216px
     card, so the tracks were forced wide, the row overflowed by 24px and the
     last permittivity box ran under the border. The same automatic-minimum
     trap as .efd-param-pair and .efd-param-trio, which were fixed and this
     was not. */
  :global(.efd-layer-fields) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xs);
  }
  :global(.efd-layer-fields > label) { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.2rem; min-width: 0; }
  :global(.efd-layer-fields > label > .param-number) { width: 100%; min-width: 0; }
  :global(.efd-layer-fields > label > span) {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* Two short numeric fields on one row: the parameter list is long, and a
     stacked label-per-row layout pushed the Run button off a laptop screen. */
  .efd-param-pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-xs); }
  /* Three short numbers across a 216px card. `align-items: end` is what keeps
     the three boxes level when one label wraps -- "beta = v_d/v_F" does, and a
     fixed min-height on the labels would only move the problem to the next
     label that is a line longer. Same trick as lab.css's .mini-row. */
  .efd-param-trio {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    align-items: end;
  }
  .efd-param-pair > label,
  .efd-param-trio > label { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.2rem; }
  .efd-param-pair > label > span,
  .efd-param-trio > label > span { font-size: 0.6875rem; color: var(--text-soft); font-weight: 600; }
  /* .param-number is a fixed 5rem, which overflows a third of this card. */
  .efd-param-pair > label > .param-number,
  .efd-param-trio > label > .param-number { width: 100%; }
  .efd-param-pair[hidden], .efd-param-trio[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* Label, number and slider on one line, after the graphene calculator's
     .gr-ef-field. `.param` is a flex COLUMN, so without the direction here the
     number's flex basis resolves down the CROSS axis: the box comes out 3.2rem
     tall and full width with the slider stranded beneath it. materials.astro
     documents the same trap for `.field.gr-ef-field`. */
  .param.efd-slider-row { flex-direction: row; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem; }
  /* A fixed basis rather than auto, so "W (nm)" and "E_F (eV)" put their
     numbers in the same column and the two rows read as a pair. */
  .efd-slider-row > label {
    flex: 0 0 3.4rem;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
  /* `min-width: 0` is load-bearing. A flex item's automatic minimum size is its
     min-content size, and an <input> with no `size` attribute reports 20
     characters -- 205px here. With flex-shrink 0 that minimum beat the 3.2rem
     basis outright: the box came out 205px wide and pushed itself and the
     slider 71px past the edge of the card. */
  /* 3.9rem, not 3.2: E_F takes a minus sign, and "-1.25" was clipped. The
     slider is the flexible item, so widening this narrows that. */
  .efd-slider-row > .efd-slider-num { flex: 0 0 3.9rem; width: 3.9rem; min-width: 0; }
  /* The native range with accent-color, which is what the graphene calculator
     uses and what .param-slider is not: accent-color fills the track to the
     left of the thumb, so the control shows its value. The flat 6px
     .param-slider bar drew the same grey line at W = 10 as at W = 200, and its
     16px thumb overhung a 6px track into the row below. */
  .efd-slider-row > .gr-slider { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 2.5rem; margin: 0; }

  /* Derived, not entered: dashed and muted, with .param-number's metrics so
     the two cells of the Discretization row are the same height. */
  .efd-derived {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0.16rem 0.38rem;
    border: 1px dashed var(--bg-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: transparent;
    font-family: var(--font);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    text-align: right;
    cursor: default;
  }
  /* The rule above gives every label in a pair a display of its own, which
     beats the UA's `[hidden] { display: none }` — so hiding the eps-below field
     by attribute does nothing without this. Same trap as .param. */
  .efd-param-pair > label[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* The top band repeats .widget-layout's own grid, so Excitation sits exactly
     over the parameters column and Observable exactly over the results one.
     Copying the track sizes rather than inventing them keeps the two aligned if
     --sidebar-w is ever retuned. */
  .efd-topbar {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: var(--sidebar-w, 300px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    align-items: end;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }
  /* Deliberately NOT .excitation-bar: that shared class is capped at 300px,
     which is right for a single column of tabs over the parameters and wrong
     here, where the band spans both columns. */

  /* Eight observables is more than one row holds on a laptop, so the tabs wrap
     rather than scrolling horizontally. */
  .efd-obs-tabs { flex-wrap: wrap; }

  /* lab.css gives .param (and the toolbars) a display of their own, and an
     author display rule beats the UA's `[hidden] { display: none }`. Hiding one
     by attribute therefore does nothing at all unless this is here — which is
     how the dipole axis stayed on screen under a plane wave. */
  .param[hidden],
  .chart-toolbar[hidden],
  .chart-block[hidden],
  .export-bar[hidden],
  .efd-obs-only[hidden],
  .efd-param-pair[hidden],
  .efd-param-trio[hidden],
  .efd-stack[hidden],
  .efd-ribbon-group[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* In the chart header, beside the title, like the axis pick in the range
     header. .chart-header is already a space-between flex row. */
  .efd-chart-pick { display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .efd-chart-pick[hidden] { display: none; }
  .efd-chart-check {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    cursor: pointer;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .efd-chart-check[hidden] { display: none; }
  .efd-chart-check input { accent-color: var(--primary); cursor: pointer; margin: 0; }

  .efd-nf-toolbar { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-xs); flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .efd-nf-toolbar .toolbar-label {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .efd-nf-toolbar .param-select { width: auto; min-width: 7rem; }
  /* No `margin-left: auto`. It was there to push the only trailing button to
     the far end; now the energy box owns that job, and on a toolbar narrow
     enough to wrap, auto handed every pixel of line one's slack to this button
     and flung it away from the Log/Linear pill it is meant to sit beside. */
  .efd-nf-all { flex: 0 0 auto; }

  /* lab.css gives .plot-tabs `display: flex`, an author rule, which beats the
     UA's `[hidden] { display: none }` -- so `scale.hidden = true` for a signed
     component never hid anything, and the page offered a Log/Linear choice
     that drawNearFieldInto ignores. Third time this page has hit this trap;
     the other two are .param and .efd-param-pair > label. */
  .plot-tabs[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* Defined in mie-scattering.astro and electron.astro, each inside its own
     scoped block, so the class did nothing here and both toolbar pills were
     rendering at the full-size .plot-tabs metrics. Same two lines as theirs. */
  .plot-tabs--toolbar { padding: 2px; }
  /* :global on the child. The nonlinear menu's buttons are injected, so they
     carry no Astro scope attribute, and a scoped descendant selector would
     style the near-field pills and skip these. */
  .plot-tabs--toolbar :global(.plot-tab) { padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem; font-size: 0.72rem; }

  .efd-geometry-card { gap: var(--space-sm); }

  .efd-dialog__actions { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-xs); }
  .efd-dialog__meta { font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--text-soft); }
  .efd-field-dialog .efd-dialog__panel { width: min(1180px, 95vw); }
  .efd-field-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    grid-template-areas:
      "rex rey rez"
      "imx imy imz"
      ".   mag .";
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .efd-field-grid .field-tile canvas { width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 1.6 / 1; height: auto; display: block; }
  .efd-field-grid .field-tile__header { margin-bottom: 0.15rem; }
  .efd-field-grid .field-tile__label { font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-soft); }
  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .efd-field-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      grid-template-areas: "rex" "imx" "rey" "imy" "rez" "imz" "mag";
    }
  }
  .efd-obs-tabs .plot-tab[hidden] { display: none; }
  .efd-src-note { font-size: 0.6875rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--text-soft); margin: 0; }

  /* `.params-head` is styled only inside mie-scattering's scoped block, so on
     this page it was a plain div until now. These two rules are what make the
     Run button sit beside the heading rather than under it. */
  .params-head { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--space-sm); }
  .params-head h3 { margin: 0; }

  .efd-run-btn { flex: 0 0 auto; font-weight: 700; }
  .efd-run-btn[disabled] { opacity: 0.55; cursor: progress; }

  /* "The numbers on screen are not the numbers you have dialled in." The busy
     half of this is already the card's spectrum rim; only staleness was
     missing, and the label cannot carry it because refreshLive rewrites the
     label wholesale on every keystroke. So it lives on a class. */
  .efd-run-btn.is-stale {
    border-color: var(--warning);
    background: var(--warning-soft);
    color: var(--warning-ink);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(217, 119, 6, 0.16);
  }
  .efd-run-btn.is-stale:hover { border-color: var(--warning); color: var(--warning-ink); }
  /* The dark block overrides neither --warning-soft nor --warning-ink, so a
     cream fill would stay cream on a dark card. */
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .efd-run-btn.is-stale {
    background: rgba(217, 119, 6, 0.16);
    color: var(--amber-pale);
  }

  .efd-enlarge-btn { margin-left: auto; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.3rem; }

  /* The schematic is a geometry drawing, not a plot, so it wants a wider,
     shorter frame than the default chart aspect. */
  .efd-wrap--schematic { --chart-aspect: 3.2; }
  /* Seven of them now that the geometry caption moved in here, and the shared
     four-column grid wrapped them onto two rows of full-size boxes -- which
     took more height under the drawing than the drawing itself. One row, and
     the boxes trimmed to earn it: the label and unit drop a step, the value
     comes down from 1.2rem, and the padding halves. */
  /* The rows are injected, so every selector that has to reach inside is
     :global() -- the scoped form would match the table and skip its contents,
     which is the trap this page has hit four times now. */
  .efd-table-wrap { margin-top: var(--space-sm); overflow-x: auto; }
  .efd-ribbon-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .efd-table-caption {
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  :global(.efd-ribbon-table th),
  :global(.efd-ribbon-table td) {
    padding: 0.3rem 0.5rem;
    text-align: right;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bg-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  :global(.efd-ribbon-table th) {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    border-bottom-color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  :global(.efd-ribbon-table th:first-child),
  :global(.efd-ribbon-table td:first-child) { text-align: left; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text); }
  :global(.efd-ribbon-table td) { color: var(--primary); font-weight: 650; }
  :global(.efd-ribbon-table tr:last-child td) { border-bottom: none; }

  .efd-readout {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .efd-readout b { color: var(--primary); font-weight: 750; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .efd-readout i { font-style: normal; }

  .efd-boot { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.4rem; padding: var(--space-sm) 0 var(--space-md); }
  .efd-boot[hidden] { display: none; }
  .efd-boot__bar { height: 4px; border-radius: var(--radius-full); background: var(--bg-muted); overflow: hidden; }
  .efd-boot__fill { height: 100%; width: 0%; background: var(--primary); transition: width 0.3s ease; }
  .efd-boot__text { font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--text-muted); }

  .efd-notes { margin-top: var(--space-md); padding-top: var(--space-md); border-top: 1px solid var(--bg-muted); }
  .efd-notes[hidden] { display: none; }
  .efd-notes ul { list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.3rem; }
  /* :global() throughout this block. showNotes builds these <li> elements with
     createElement, so they carry no data-astro-cid attribute and a scoped
     selector could never match one -- every note has been rendering at
     inherited body metrics. Same for the error box, which showError creates. */
  :global(.efd-notes li) {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    padding-left: 0.85rem;
    position: relative;
  }
  :global(.efd-notes li::before) { content: '·'; position: absolute; left: 0.2rem; font-weight: 800; }
  :global(.efd-notes li.efd-note--warn) { color: var(--warning); }

  :global(.efd-error) {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    padding: var(--space-sm);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--danger);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--danger-soft);
    color: var(--danger);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  :global(.efd-error[hidden]) { display: none; }

  /* Enlarged geometry dialog, after the cylinder page's dispersion dialog. */
  .efd-dialog { border: none; padding: 0; background: transparent; max-width: 96vw; max-height: 94vh; }
  .efd-dialog::backdrop { background: rgba(15, 16, 32, 0.62); }
  .efd-dialog__panel {
    width: min(1180px, 94vw);
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--bg-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: var(--space-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .efd-dialog__header { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--space-sm); }
  .efd-dialog__header h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1rem; }
  .efd-dialog__canvas-box { width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 2.4; position: relative; }
  .efd-dialog__canvas-box canvas { width: 100%; height: 100%; display: block; }
  .efd-dialog__caption { font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--text-soft); line-height: 1.5; margin: 0; }

  /* KaTeX. Injected markup carries no Astro scope hash, so these have to be
     global to reach it at all. The display equations are sized down a little:
     at full size the drift tensor overflowed its column and grew a horizontal
     scrollbar, and a couple of points is all it needed to fit. */
  .notes-card :global(.katex) { color: var(--text); font-size: 1em; }
  /* MEASURE THESE WITH A CLONE, not with getBoundingClientRect on the .katex
     node. KaTeX's .katex and .katex-html compute to `display: block` here, so
     they report the CONTAINER's width whatever the formula's own width is --
     which made the drift tensor look 98% full at 770px when a clone measured
     in an unconstrained probe puts it at 353px, 46%. Three separate probes
     said "no overflow" for the same reason before that one said anything true.
     So the size is left alone at desktop: nothing was measured to be tight.
     The extra margin and padding are for the vertical crowding against the
     prose, and the step down below 900px is for the narrow card, where the
     slack is real but smaller. `overflow-x: auto` stays as the net. */
  .note-eq { margin: var(--space-md) 0; padding: 0.25rem 0; overflow-x: auto; }
  .note-eq :global(.katex-display) { margin: 0; padding: 0.15rem 0; }
  .note-eq :global(.katex-display) > :global(.katex) { text-align: left; font-size: 0.94em; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .note-eq :global(.katex-display) > :global(.katex) { font-size: 0.84em; }
  }
  dd :global(.katex) { color: inherit; }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .widget-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .efd-topbar { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .module-bar { --module-cols: 1; }
    .efd-param-pair, .efd-param-trio { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .efd-stats { --stats-cols: 2; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { initElectrostaticsFd } from '../../scripts/electrostatics-fd/main';
  import { initPermalinks } from '../../lib/permalink';

  initElectrostaticsFd();

  // THE WHOLE PARAMETER BOX, so that opening a shared link and pressing Run
  // reproduces the figure. Only what was CHANGED is written -- initPermalinks
  // drops any field sitting at its own default -- so the common link is still
  // a handful of parameters, and a fully dialled-in one is long because it
  // has to be. Mirrors are left out on purpose: the beam energy in keV and the
  // wavelength beside the near-field energy are second faces of v/c and of
  // that energy, and carrying both invites the two to disagree.
  //
  // ORDER IS THE CONTRACT. The restore loop applies these in sequence, so a
  // field that decides how many of the later ones exist, or what their
  // numbers mean, is listed before them.
  const link: Record<string, string> = {
    // The counts first: they build the ribbon cards and the layer rows.
    n: 'efd-nribbons', nl: 'efd-nlayers',
    // Then the unit, before any energy lands in a box that it governs.
    u: 'efd-xunit',
  };
  // Every ribbon, not just the first two: five cards of eight numbers is what
  // the box actually holds, and a link that carried three of them was a link
  // that quietly changed the structure.
  for (let j = 0; j < 5; j += 1) {
    link[`w${j}`] = `efd-w-${j}`;
    link[`x${j}`] = `efd-x0-${j}`;
    link[`z${j}`] = `efd-z0-${j}`;
    link[`l${j}`] = `efd-l-${j}`;
    link[`ef${j}`] = `efd-ef-${j}`;
    link[`g${j}`] = `efd-gamma-${j}`;
    link[`b${j}`] = `efd-beta-${j}`;
    link[`t${j}`] = `efd-temp-${j}`;
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < 8; i += 1) {
    link[`ld${i}`] = `efd-lay-${i}-d`;
    link[`lp${i}`] = `efd-lay-${i}-par`;
    link[`lq${i}`] = `efd-lay-${i}-perp`;
  }
  Object.assign(link, {
    // Environment, grids, and how many of each thing to solve for.
    epsa: 'efd-eps-a', epsb: 'efd-eps-b', eps: 'efd-sub-eps',
    emin: 'efd-e-min', emax: 'efd-e-max', en: 'efd-e-n',
    q: 'efd-q', qmin: 'efd-q-min', qmax: 'efd-q-max', qn: 'efd-q-npos',
    nm: 'efd-nmodes', nb: 'efd-nbands', np: 'efd-npeaks',
    // The drive's own parameters, and the near field's.
    dx: 'efd-dip-x', dz: 'efd-dip-z', dp: 'efd-dip-p',
    zb: 'efd-b', vc: 'efd-vc',
    nfe: 'efd-nf-energy', nfx: 'efd-nf-nx', nfz: 'efd-nf-nz',
    nfc: 'efd-nf-comp', nfs: 'efd-nf-source',
    // The LDOS map's own peak tracks: display only, so restoring it costs a
    // redraw. Its neighbour `efd-ldos-bands` is deliberately NOT here -- that
    // box starts a second calculation when it is ticked, and a link that
    // silently launched a solve on load is a different kind of thing from a
    // link that remembers what you were looking at.
    lpk: 'efd-ldos-peaks',
    // LAST, and `src` before `view`: the drive governs which observables
    // exist, so it has to be in force before the view lands, and both want
    // the restored numbers already in the boxes when they trigger a redraw.
    src: 'efd-src', view: 'efd-view',
  });
  initPermalinks(link);
</script>
